course: world
lesson: 26
title: Food chains and food webs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A food chain shows who eats whom, and so how energy passes from one living thing to the next: grass → rabbit → fox.
explain: Every food chain starts with a producer, a plant or alga that makes its own food from sunlight; the animals that eat are consumers.
explain: An animal that hunts other animals is a predator and the animal it hunts is its prey; herbivores eat plants, carnivores eat animals, omnivores eat both.
explain: Decomposers, such as fungi, bacteria and worms, break down dead plants and animals and return their minerals to the soil for new plants.
explain: In nature most animals eat more than one thing and are eaten by more than one thing, so the chains join into a food web, like a net.
fact: A food chain shows how food and energy pass from one living thing to another.
fact: Every food chain begins with a producer, such as a green plant or an alga.
fact: Producers make their own food using the energy of sunlight.
fact: Consumers are living things that get their food by eating other living things.
fact: Herbivores eat plants, carnivores eat animals, and omnivores eat both.
fact: A predator hunts and eats other animals, called its prey.
fact: Decomposers break down dead plants and animals and return minerals to the soil.
fact: Fungi, such as mushrooms and moulds, bacteria, which are living things far too small to see, and earthworms are decomposers.
fact: The arrows in a food chain point from the thing eaten to the thing that eats it.
fact: A food web is many food chains joined together.
fact: The energy in almost every food chain comes first from the Sun.
exercise: What does every food chain start with? | A producer such as a plant | A large and hungry predator | A decomposer such as a fungus | A herbivore such as a rabbit
exercise: Why are plants called producers? | They make their own food from sunlight | They produce the most seeds | They produce soil for animals | They are eaten by every animal
exercise: In the chain grass → rabbit → fox, what is the rabbit? | A herbivore and prey | A producer | A predator of the fox | A decomposer
exercise: In the chain grass → rabbit → fox, what is the fox? | A predator | A producer | A herbivore | Prey of the rabbit
exercise: What does an arrow in a food chain mean? | Is eaten by | Is bigger than | Lives next to | Is afraid of
exercise: What do decomposers do? | Break down dead things and return minerals to soil | Hunt live animals for their food | Make their own food from sunlight in their green leaves | Carry pollen from flower to flower
exercise: Which of these is a decomposer? | A mushroom | A rabbit | A hawk | A sunflower
exercise: What is an omnivore? | An animal that eats plants and animals | An animal that eats only plants | An animal that eats only meat | An animal that eats dead leaves only
exercise: Where does the energy in a food chain come from first? | The Sun | The soil | The water | The predator
exercise: What is a food web? | Many food chains joined together | A spider's web used to catch food | A list of plants in a garden | A single chain with one animal
exercise: Why is a food web a better picture of nature than one chain? | Most animals eat and are eaten by several others | Food webs are easier to draw | Animals only ever eat one kind of food all their lives | Plants eat animals in a web
exercise: Which of these is a herbivore? | A deer | A wolf | A shark | An owl
exercise: Why are decomposers important to plants? | They return minerals to the soil | They make sunlight for plants | They eat the plants' pests | They carry water to the roots
exercise: Which animal is both a predator and prey? | A frog that eats flies and is eaten by herons | A lion that no other animal ever hunts or eats | A cow that eats grass only | A tree that is eaten by deer
test: In a pond, algae → tadpoles → small fish → heron. Which is the producer? | The algae | The tadpoles | The small fish | The heron
test: Why would a forest floor be covered with mountains of dead leaves if there were no decomposers? | Nothing would break the leaves down | Trees would stop dropping leaves | Animals would eat all the leaves | Leaves would turn back into trees
test: A bear eats berries, fish and honey. What kind of eater is it? | An omnivore | A herbivore | A carnivore | A producer
test: Which chain is drawn correctly? | Leaf → caterpillar → bird | Bird → caterpillar → leaf | Caterpillar → leaf → bird | Leaf → bird → caterpillar
test: An owl eats mice. What are the mice to the owl? | Its prey | Its predator | Its producer | Its decomposer
test: Why can no food chain start with a cat? | A cat cannot make its own food | A cat is too big to be eaten | A cat only eats plants | A cat lives in houses
test: Which is true about mushrooms? | They feed on dead material and break it down | They make food from sunlight like plants | They hunt insects as predators | They are the producers of the forest
test: A lion eats a zebra that ate grass. Where did the lion's energy first come from? | The Sun | The zebra's muscles | The water hole | The lion's own body
test: Hawks eat both snakes and mice, and snakes also eat mice. What does this make? | A food web | A single food chain | A life cycle | A habitat
test: wot does every food chain start with | A green plant or another producer | A big hungry predator, like a wolf | A decomposer, like a mushroom or worm | A plant-eating animal, like a rabbit
test: what's a decomposer then | A living thing that breaks down dead plants and animals | An animal that hunts down and eats other animals for its food | A plant that makes its own food | An animal that eats only plants
test: Tom says in a food chain the arrow points to what gets eaten. true? | No, it points from the eaten to the eater | Yes, it points at the food | Yes, it shows who runs away | No, the arrows have no direction
test: Mia reckons the energy in food chains comes from the soil. right? | No, almost all of it comes first from the Sun | Yes, plants get their energy from soil | Yes, decomposers make all the energy | No, it comes first from the animals at the top
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a producer within a food chain? | A living thing that manufactures its own food by the energy of sunlight | A living thing that consumes only animals | A living thing that decomposes dead matter | A living thing that consumes plants and animals alike, as a bear or a human does
exam: Identify the direction indicated by the arrows of a food chain. | From the thing eaten to the thing that eats it | From the predator to its prey | From the decomposer to the producer | From the largest animal to the smallest
exam: Identify, from among the following, a decomposer. | A fungus | A rabbit | A fox | A grass
exam: Identify the original source of the energy in almost every food chain. | The Sun | The soil | Water | The decomposers
